  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe Bermuda High builds westward toward the Eastern Seaboard \nthis week, bringing warmer and more humid conditions by mid to \nlate week. Temperatures generally reach into the mid 80s by \nWednesday, with upper 80s to low 90s expected for most areas \nfrom Thursday through at least Saturday. Dew points will also be\non the rise through the week, and likely begin to push 70 \ndegrees by Thursday, and expand on Friday and the weekend.\n\nThe combination of heat and humidity likely pushes heat indices\ninto the low to mid 90s across parts of southern New Hampshire \nand just back from the coast of Maine. The heat overall doesn't \nlook like a high end event, but an extended period of multiple \ndays with heat indices into the low to mid 90s.\n\nCooler temperatures likely don't arrive until early to mid week\nnext week with a cold front. There remains a widespread time \nrange on the possible arrival of this front anywhere from late \nMonday to Wednesday of next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA long wave trough over the Canadian Prairies will gradually \ndeepen and slide east the second half of the week. Ensembles are\nin decent agreement that southwest flow ahead of the trough \nwill advect deep moisture into the region with PWATs nearing 2.0\ninches by Thursday. The trough axis does not cross the area \nthrough the weekend allowing for deep moisture to persist. Dew \npoints in the upper 60s to low 70s Thursday through Saturday \ncombined with surface heating will likely generate sufficient \ninstability for thunderstorms during the afternoons. Ensembles \nare highlighting Friday and Saturday as the days with high \nprobabilities of CAPE greater than 500 J/kg across much of the \narea with CAPE mainly focused over NH Thursday. Deep layer \nshear through the period may approach 35 kts, which will be \nsufficient for organized convection. The lack of a well defined \nsurface feature for forcing and ridging just to the east \nsuggests coverage of any showers and storms will be limited \nThursday and Friday. Embedded short waves help knock the ridge \ndown Saturday with better forcing for ascent overspreading the \narea. Saturday is when PoPs will climb into the 50 to 70 percent\nrange south to north with PoPs lowering into next week. Machine\nLearning guidance out of CSU is highlighting potential for \nstrong to severe storms Friday through Sunday with the highest \nprobabilities on Saturday. It will likely take a few more model \ncycles to pin down the finer details on storms Friday into the \nweekend while there does appear some potential for torrential \nrainfall given the high PWATs and strong to severe storms.",
